 "statements": {
  "electric-potential": "The electric potential is the potential energy per unit charge, $V = \\text{PE}/q$. Since the work done by the field, and hence the change in potential energy, is proportional to the test charge, the dependence on the charge cancels and $V$ does not depend on $q$.",
  "potential-difference": "The potential difference between points A and B is the change in potential energy of a charge moved from A to B, divided by the charge. Its unit, the joule per coulomb, is the volt, named after Alessandro Volta. Voltage is the common name for potential difference, and a voltage always means the potential difference between two points. Voltage is not the same as energy: a motorcycle battery and a car battery can both be 12 V, yet one stores much more energy than the other.",
  "capacitance": "Capacitance is the amount of charge a capacitor stores per volt applied across it, $C = Q/V$. The greater the voltage applied to any capacitor, the greater the charge stored in it, and different capacitors store different charges for the same voltage. The unit of capacitance, the farad, is a coulomb per volt, a very large capacitance, and typical capacitors run from picofarads to millifarads.",
  "voltage-across-uniform-field": "In a uniform electric field the voltage between two points is the field strength times the distance between them along the field, $V_\\text{AB} = Ed$, so the field is the voltage divided by the distance. The work the field does on a positive charge moved from the positive plate to the negative plate is both $qV_\\text{AB}$ and $qEd$, and the charge cancels."
 },
 "new": [
